; hazard-subject-smc.a -- the planted self-modifying-code construction.
;
; WHY AN OPCODE-BYTE PATCH, NOT THE TEXTBOOK OPERAND PATCH
; ---------------------------------------------------------
; The obvious self-modifying idiom -- an `inc`/`dec` that rewrites the
; immediate operand of a nearby `lda #` -- changes a VALUE. A detector that
; only asks "did a store land on an operand byte" catches that shape and
; nothing else. This routine plants the harder case on purpose: a store of a
; literal opcode byte onto another instruction's OPCODE position, so the
; failure mode is a CONTROL-FLOW change rather than a value change. Relocating
; this routine by even one byte moves the address the store targets, and
; nothing in a disassembly listing of either the original or the moved copy
; shows that the two no longer agree -- the listings look identical; only the
; runtime behaviour differs.
;
; THE SHAPE
; ---------
; `hazard_smc_entry` is a short self-terminating loop. On the first pass it
; writes an observable value to the border-colour register (so the effect is
; visible on a real screen and readable back out of memory, not dead code),
; then arms itself by storing the literal byte $60 -- the 6510's `rts` opcode
; -- directly over `smc_patch_target`, which is the OPCODE BYTE of the `nop`
; immediately below this comment. On the second pass through the same loop,
; the CPU no longer executes a `nop` at that address: it executes `rts`, and
; the routine returns exactly where the first pass simply fell through to the
; next instruction. The label `smc_patch_target` is defined as `*` on the line
; immediately before its host instruction, so the address is nameable without
; an absolute literal anywhere in this file.

hazard_smc_entry
smc_patch_target = *
        nop                       ; host instruction: its own opcode byte is
                                   ; overwritten with $60 (rts) below
        lda #$01
        sta $d020                 ; observable: border colour changes on every pass
        lda #$60
        sta smc_patch_target      ; arm: plant the rts opcode over the host
                                   ; instruction's own opcode byte
        jmp hazard_smc_entry
